//
// A METHOD CALL on a nullable collection names the `?`, not a method that plainly exists.
//
// This is the sharper of the two, because of WHERE it lands: the method dispatch matched no
// `τ?` arm, so the STRUCT-FIELD fallback took over and named the CONTENT def — *"Unknown field
// vector.remove"*.  The reader is told a method they can read in STDLIB.md does not exist, and
// sent to look for a misspelling, while the fault is one character wide and on the receiver.
//
// The refusal itself is right — `(N-Coal)` gives a `τ?` no implicit unwrap — so this is
// loft#1453's cure (`for` / `map` / `filter`) carried out of its one home to a site it did
// not reach.  The sibling spelling `rev(c)` is `1450b`; separate files because a firing
// `@EXPECT_ERROR` stops the run.
//
// Axes it HOLDS FIXED:  the receiver is DECLARED `vector<…>?`, so the refusal is reachable
//                       today without loft#1450's `(N-Prop)` leg.
//
// @falsified-at: 8ba3e755 — reports *"Unknown field vector.remove"* there, so the
//   `@EXPECT_ERROR` misses and the file fails.

struct Bag1450c { xs: vector<integer>? }

// @EXPECT_ERROR: cannot call `.remove` on vector<integer>? because it is NULLABLE
fn remove_from_it(b: Bag1450c) {
  b.xs.remove(0);
}
